.TH CHMAP_INSERT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_insert \- insert a key-value pair (type-safe)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chashmap.h>

chmap_insert(hm_name, key, val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-safe wrapper for chmap_insert_elem() that builds the cmap_pair for
the key and the value from the expressions passed. If the key already
exists the value is updated silently. Calls ccol_fatal_err() only on a
true failure (ccol_not_enough_memory, ccol_container_full, or
ccol_invalid_args).

key and val must be of the map's declared key and value types. Both are
stored as the raw bytes of the expression passed, sized with sizeof,
rather than converted to the declared type the way a plain C assignment
would convert them.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Hash map to insert into
key      Key to insert
val      Value to associate with key
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Terminates program only on true failure, not on existing-key update
Automatically takes address of key and value
Handles both value types and string types correctly
For a float or double key, equality is bitwise except that -0.0 and
0.0 are canonicalized to the same key; NaN keys follow bitwise
equality, not IEEE 754 equality (see chmap_construct(3))
For a long double key, equality is value-based (native ==) rather
than bitwise, unlike every other key type; every NaN long double
collapses into a single key (see chmap_construct(3))
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(map, int, char*);
int first = 42, second = 100;
chmap_insert(map, first, "hello");
chmap_insert(map, second, "world");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR chmap_get (3),
.BR chmap_remove (3)
